    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<div class="key-takeaways"><h2>Key takeaways</h2><ul>
<li>Many Texas policies use <strong>percentage deductibles</strong> for wind, hail, or hurricanes, usually a percentage of Coverage A (dwelling limit), not of the damage.</li>
<li>A 2% deductible on a home insured for $250,000 is $5,000 out of pocket per qualifying loss.</li>
<li>Named storm (hurricane) deductibles apply only to storms that meet the policy's definition; wind/hail deductibles apply to any wind or hail loss.</li>
<li>TWIA calculates its deductible from the coverage limit, with separate deductibles for the dwelling and personal property.</li>
<li>One hurricane can mean two or three deductibles: TWIA, flood, and possibly homeowners.</li>
</ul></div>

<p>Few parts of a Texas property policy surprise people more than the hurricane deductible. Homeowners in Port Arthur and the rest of Southeast Texas often expect a flat $1,000 or $2,500 deductible, then learn after a storm that their wind or hurricane deductible is a percentage of the dwelling limit. On a typical home, that can mean several thousand dollars before the insurer pays anything.</p>

<p>This article explains how hurricane deductibles in Texas work, how named storm deductibles differ from wind/hail deductibles, how TWIA deductibles are calculated, and how to run the numbers on your own policy. For quick math, use our <a href="/hurricane-deductible-calculator/">hurricane deductible calculator</a>. The only source that truly answers "what is my deductible" is your declarations page and policy, so keep them handy as you read.</p>

<h2>How a percentage hurricane deductible works</h2>

<p>A flat deductible is a fixed dollar amount, such as $1,000. A percentage deductible is a percentage of a coverage limit. On Texas homeowners policies, the percentage is usually applied to <strong>Coverage A</strong>, the dwelling limit shown on your declarations page.</p>

<p>The key point: the percentage is based on what the house is <em>insured for</em>, not on how much damage it suffered. A 2% deductible on a $300,000 dwelling limit is $6,000, whether the storm caused $8,000 of damage or $80,000.</p>

<p>Common percentages are 1%, 2%, and 5%, though options vary by insurer and policy. Many policies also set a minimum dollar amount, so the deductible is the greater of the percentage or the minimum.</p>

<h3>Why insurers use them</h3>
<p>Coastal wind risk is concentrated. One hurricane can cause thousands of claims in the same week. Percentage deductibles shift more of the first-dollar cost of a storm to policyholders and can lower premiums. For you, the trade-off is a lower premium in exchange for a larger out-of-pocket amount when a storm hits.</p>

<h2>Worked examples</h2>

<p>The examples below show how a percentage deductible changes the payout on the same loss. They assume the damage is covered and the numbers are before depreciation, policy limits, or other adjustments. Real claims are more complicated.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Coverage A (dwelling)</th><th>Deductible</th><th>Deductible in dollars</th><th>Covered damage</th><th>Payment before depreciation</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>$200,000</td><td>1%</td><td>$2,000</td><td>$15,000</td><td>$13,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>$200,000</td><td>2%</td><td>$4,000</td><td>$15,000</td><td>$11,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>$200,000</td><td>5%</td><td>$10,000</td><td>$15,000</td><td>$5,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>$300,000</td><td>2%</td><td>$6,000</td><td>$5,500</td><td>$0 (damage below deductible)</td></tr>
<tr><td>$300,000</td><td>2%</td><td>$6,000</td><td>$40,000</td><td>$34,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>$300,000</td><td>5%</td><td>$15,000</td><td>$40,000</td><td>$25,000</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>Two lessons stand out. First, moderate damage, such as a partial roof replacement, can fall entirely inside a 2% or 5% deductible on a well-insured home. Second, the difference between 1% and 5% grows with the dwelling limit. On a $300,000 home, it is the difference between $3,000 and $15,000 out of pocket.</p>

<h3>Depreciation on top of the deductible</h3>
<p>On replacement cost policies, insurers typically pay actual cash value (ACV) first and hold back recoverable depreciation until repairs are complete. So the first check can be much smaller than the "payment" column above. Some Texas roof coverage is ACV-only or uses a roof payment schedule, which can reduce the payment further. See our <a href="/claim-calculator/">claim calculator</a> to estimate how ACV, depreciation, and the deductible combine.</p>

<figure><img src="/assets/img/photos/homeowners-reviewing-settlement-with-adjuster.webp" alt="Homeowners reviewing a settlement breakdown with an adjuster" width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Your declarations page lists each deductible and the limit it is based on. Read it before storm season, not after.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>Named storm vs. wind/hail deductibles</h2>

<p>Texas policies can carry several different deductibles, and which one applies depends on what caused the damage.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Deductible type</th><th>When it usually applies</th><th>What to check</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>All other perils (AOP)</td><td>Non-wind losses, such as fire, theft, or a burst pipe</td><td>Usually a flat dollar amount</td></tr>
<tr><td>Wind/hail</td><td>Any wind or hail damage, including thunderstorms and hail</td><td>Flat or percentage; may be a percentage of Coverage A</td></tr>
<tr><td>Named storm / hurricane</td><td>Damage from a storm that meets the policy's definition (for example, a storm named by the National Hurricane Center or declared a hurricane)</td><td>Definition of "named storm" or "hurricane"; the time window it covers</td></tr>
<tr><td>Flood (NFIP or private)</td><td>Flood, storm surge, rising water</td><td>Separate building and contents deductibles</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>Definitions matter. One policy may trigger a named storm deductible for any storm the NHC names. Another may require a hurricane watch or warning for the area, or define a window of time before and after the storm. A thunderstorm that blows shingles off a roof in Groves in April would typically fall under the wind/hail deductible, not the named storm deductible. Read the deductible endorsement for your exact wording.</p>

<div class="callout callout-gold"><span class="callout-title">Port Arthur homeowners often have no wind deductible on the homeowners policy at all</span><p>Because Jefferson County is a TWIA first-tier coastal county, many Port Arthur homeowners policies exclude wind and hail entirely. In that case, wind damage goes to TWIA, and the TWIA deductible is the one that matters for wind. The homeowners deductible applies only to non-wind perils on that policy.</p></div>

[[cta]]

<h2>How TWIA deductibles work</h2>

<p>For Port Arthur, Nederland, Groves, Port Neches, Sabine Pass and the rest of Jefferson County, TWIA is often the wind and hail insurer. According to TWIA's own policyholder guidance:</p>

<ul>
<li>The deductible is <strong>calculated from the maximum limit of coverage on your property, not on the loss amount</strong>. Your declarations page shows that limit.</li>
<li>The deductible is chosen by you and your agent when the application is submitted.</li>
<li>There is a deductible for the <strong>dwelling</strong>, which includes the roof and other parts attached to the home, and a <strong>separate deductible for personal property</strong> if it is damaged.</li>
<li>TWIA subtracts the deductible from the claim payment and, on replacement cost policies, holds back depreciation until repairs are made. To be paid that depreciation, TWIA needs proof you paid your deductible to the contractor, such as a canceled check, money order, or financing agreement.</li>
<li>If you use several contractors, you still have one dwelling deductible and one personal property deductible.</li>
</ul>

<p>TWIA offers a range of deductible options, including percentage options. Which options are available, and the minimums, depend on your policy and TWIA's current rules, so confirm with your agent. Our <a href="/twia-windstorm-claim-guide-port-arthur/">TWIA windstorm claim guide</a> and <a href="/services/twia-windstorm-claims/">TWIA windstorm claims</a> page cover the rest of the TWIA process.</p>

<h3>A TWIA example</h3>
<p>Say a Port Arthur home has a TWIA dwelling limit of $220,000 with a 2% deductible ($4,400) and a contents limit of $60,000 with a 2% deductible ($1,200). A hurricane causes $28,000 in covered roof and interior damage to the structure and $3,000 in damaged contents. The dwelling payment before depreciation would be $23,600, and the contents payment would be $1,800. Those are illustrative numbers only; check your own declarations page for actual limits and deductibles.</p>

<h2>Stacking deductibles: one hurricane, several claims</h2>

<p>Because wind, flood, and other perils are often insured separately in Jefferson County, one hurricane can bring several deductibles:</p>

<ol>
<li><strong>TWIA wind deductible</strong> for roof, siding, and wind-driven rain damage.</li>
<li><strong>Flood deductible(s)</strong> for building and contents damage from rising water or surge.</li>
<li><strong>Homeowners deductible</strong> if there is a covered non-wind loss on that policy, such as certain additional living expenses or a separate peril.</li>
</ol>

<p>That is why allocation matters. When damage is pushed from the wind policy to the flood policy, or the other way around, it changes which deductible applies and whether the damage exceeds it. Our article on <a href="/wind-vs-flood-damage-port-arthur-insurance/">wind vs. flood damage in Port Arthur</a> explains how that split is made.</p>

<h2>What deductibles do not change</h2>

<ul>
<li><strong>Your duties after a loss.</strong> Even if you think damage is below the deductible, report it and document it. Hidden damage often shows up later, and a second storm in the same season may change the picture.</li>
<li><strong>The insurer's duty to estimate fully.</strong> A low estimate that lands just under the deductible deserves a careful second look. Missed items, such as decking, underlayment, drip edge, code items, or interior repairs, can push the total above it.</li>
<li><strong>Contractor rules.</strong> Texas law generally prohibits contractors from offering to waive, absorb, or rebate your deductible. A contractor promising to "cover your deductible" is a red flag.</li>
</ul>

<figure><img src="/assets/img/photos/adjuster-explaining-claim-to-couple.webp" alt="Adjuster explaining an insurance claim estimate to a couple at home" width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>When an estimate lands just under the deductible, a line-by-line review can show whether items were missed.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>How to review your hurricane deductible before storm season</h2>

<ol>
<li>Find your declarations page for every policy: homeowners, TWIA, and flood.</li>
<li>Write down each deductible and whether it is a flat amount or a percentage, and of what limit.</li>
<li>Convert percentages to dollars using the limit shown. Our <a href="/hurricane-deductible-calculator/">hurricane deductible calculator</a> does this for you.</li>
<li>Read the named storm or hurricane deductible definition, if your policy has one.</li>
<li>Decide whether you can cover that amount from savings after a storm. If not, ask your agent about other options and the premium difference.</li>
<li>Check your dwelling limit. Being underinsured lowers the deductible in dollars but can leave you short on a large loss.</li>
</ol>

<h2>When a public adjuster can help</h2>

<p>A deductible is fixed by the policy; a public adjuster cannot change it. What a Port Arthur Public Adjuster can do is make sure the full scope of covered damage is documented and priced, so the deductible is applied to an accurate number. On a claim where the insurer's estimate barely clears, or does not clear, the deductible, a careful review of the scope may show whether items were left out. A public adjuster can also help sort out which deductible applies when damage is split between TWIA, flood, and homeowners policies.</p>

<p>Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement. No one can promise a specific result. If you are unsure whether your hurricane claim was estimated correctly, a Port Arthur Public Adjuster can review it through our <a href="/services/insurance-estimate-review/">insurance estimate review</a> service.</p>

<h2>The bottom line</h2>

<p>In Texas, the hurricane deductible is often a percentage of your dwelling limit, which can turn a "small" percentage into thousands of dollars. Know each deductible on each policy, convert it to dollars before the season starts, and make sure any storm claim is estimated in full so the deductible is subtracted from the right number.</p>
